Question 1

Your team manages a microservices application on GKE. One of the services is experiencing intermittent high latency that is difficult to reproduce. You need to identify the root cause across multiple service calls. What should you do?

A. Add log statements throughout the service code and export logs to BigQuery for analysis.
B. Enable Cloud Trace and instrument the service code to capture distributed trace data.
C. Create a Cloud Monitoring dashboard with custom metrics for the service.
D. Configure Cloud Profiler to capture CPU usage of the service.

Correct answer
B. Enable Cloud Trace and instrument the service code to capture distributed trace data.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. Log statements can help with debugging but do not provide a correlated view of latency across service boundaries, making it hard to pinpoint which call is causing the latency.
B: Correct. Cloud Trace is designed for distributed tracing. It provides a correlated view of how requests flow through multiple services, making it ideal for identifying latency bottlenecks across service calls.
C: Incorrect. Custom metrics can surface that latency is high but cannot show you which downstream call is responsible across distributed services.
D: Incorrect. Cloud Profiler analyzes CPU and memory usage within a single process. It does not trace requests across service boundaries.

https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview
