Question 2

Your organization uses Cloud Build to build container images and push them to Artifact Registry. You want to ensure that only container images that have passed a vulnerability scan can be deployed to your GKE production cluster. What should you do?

A. Configure Cloud Build to run a vulnerability scan step and fail the build if critical vulnerabilities are found.
B. Enable Binary Authorization on the GKE cluster and configure an attestation policy that requires a vulnerability scan attestation before deployment.
C. Use Cloud Armor to block deployments that originate from untrusted container images.
D. Enable Container Analysis on Artifact Registry and configure a Cloud Monitoring alert when vulnerabilities are discovered.

Correct answer
B. Enable Binary Authorization on the GKE cluster and configure an attestation policy that requires a vulnerability scan attestation before deployment.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. Failing the build prevents a vulnerable image from being pushed, but it does not prevent a previously built or manually pushed image from being deployed to the cluster.
B: Correct. Binary Authorization enforces a policy at deploy time that requires an attestation (a cryptographic signature) proving the image passed a vulnerability scan. This is the recommended way to enforce supply chain security at the cluster level.
C: Incorrect. Cloud Armor is a network security service. It cannot inspect or block container image deployments.
D: Incorrect. A Cloud Monitoring alert notifies you of vulnerabilities after the fact but does not prevent a vulnerable image from being deployed.

https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview
https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/vulnerability-scanning-kritis

