Question 3

Your team runs a stateless web application on Cloud Run. You want to implement a deployment strategy that allows you to roll back to the previous version within seconds if a problem is detected. What should you do?

A. Use Cloud Deploy to manage the deployment and configure a canary strategy.
B. Deploy the new revision with traffic split, keeping the previous revision active. Roll back by shifting 100% of traffic back to the previous revision.
C. Keep a backup container image in Artifact Registry and redeploy it using Cloud Build when a rollback is needed.
D. Deploy the new revision and use Cloud Monitoring to trigger a Cloud Function that restarts the old revision on error.

Correct answer
B. Deploy the new revision with traffic split, keeping the previous revision active. Roll back by shifting 100% of traffic back to the previous revision.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. Cloud Deploy canary strategies can be useful but involve additional pipeline stages and are more complex to configure for a simple, fast rollback scenario.
B: Correct. Cloud Run keeps previous revisions active and allows instant traffic shifting between them. Rolling back is simply redirecting 100% of traffic back to the prior revision, which takes effect in seconds.
C: Incorrect. Re-triggering a Cloud Build pipeline adds unnecessary latency to a rollback operation and introduces more potential points of failure.
D: Incorrect. This is overly complex and unreliable. Cloud Run's built-in traffic splitting is simpler and faster for rollbacks.

https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration
