Question 5

Your company's SRE team has defined an SLO of 99.5% availability per rolling 30-day window for a critical API. At the start of the current window the error budget is fully intact. Midway through the window a major incident consumes 60% of the error budget. The development team wants to continue releasing new features. What should you do?

A. Continue releasing features, but implement more thorough automated testing before each release.
B. Halt feature releases and focus engineering effort on reliability improvements until the error budget recovers.
C. Ask the customer to relax the SLO temporarily so feature releases can continue.
D. Increase the SLO target to 99.9% to create a larger error budget.

Correct answer
B. Halt feature releases and focus engineering effort on reliability improvements until the error budget recovers.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. While improved testing is valuable, continuing to release features when the error budget is significantly consumed violates SRE error budget policy, which recommends prioritizing reliability work when the budget is spent.
B: Correct. SRE error budget policy recommends halting risky changes such as feature releases when the error budget is significantly consumed. This aligns incentives between reliability and feature velocity.
C: Incorrect. Asking customers to relax SLOs reactively after budget burn is not a recommended SRE practice. SLOs should be negotiated proactively.
D: Incorrect. Increasing the SLO target makes the SLO stricter, which would make the error budget smaller, not larger.

