Question 7

Your organization wants to ensure that container images stored in Artifact Registry do not contain known HIGH or CRITICAL vulnerabilities before they are promoted to production. You want this check to happen automatically as part of the CI pipeline. What should you do?

A. Enable Artifact Registry vulnerability scanning and add a Cloud Build step that queries the scan results and fails the build if HIGH or CRITICAL vulnerabilities are found.
B. Configure a Cloud Monitoring alert to notify the team when Artifact Registry detects a HIGH or CRITICAL vulnerability.
C. Enable Binary Authorization and configure it to block images with known vulnerabilities from being pulled.
D. Periodically run the gcloud artifacts docker images scan command and email the results to the security team.

Correct answer
A. Enable Artifact Registry vulnerability scanning and add a Cloud Build step that queries the scan results and fails the build if HIGH or CRITICAL vulnerabilities are found.

Feedback
A: Correct. Enabling vulnerability scanning on Artifact Registry allows the scan to happen automatically on push. Adding a build step that checks and fails on HIGH/CRITICAL findings integrates the gate directly into the CI pipeline before promotion.
B: Incorrect. A monitoring alert notifies the team after the image has already been stored, but does not block the promotion pipeline automatically.
C: Incorrect. Binary Authorization enforces policy at deploy time, not at the CI pipeline stage. It is a complementary control but does not automate the CI check.
D: Incorrect. Running a manual or periodic scan and emailing results is not automated and does not block a promotion pipeline.

