Question 8

Your company runs a large GKE workload. Operations engineers have noticed that many Pods are being OOMKilled due to memory limits that are set too low. You need a Google-recommended, low-effort way to automatically tune resource requests and limits over time. What should you do?

A. Manually review metrics from Cloud Monitoring and update resource requests in each Deployment manifest.
B. Enable the Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) in recommendation mode, review suggestions, and apply them to your Deployment manifests.
C. Enable the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) with CPU and memory metrics to scale out Pods when memory is constrained.
D. Configure Node Auto Provisioning to add nodes with more memory to the cluster.

Correct answer
B. Enable the Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) in recommendation mode, review suggestions, and apply them to your Deployment manifests.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. Manually reviewing metrics and editing manifests is labor-intensive and does not scale well across many Deployments.
B: Correct. VPA analyzes historical resource utilization and recommends (or automatically applies) appropriate CPU and memory requests and limits. Using it in recommendation mode first gives operators a chance to review changes before applying them.
C: Incorrect. HPA scales the number of Pod replicas based on metrics, but it does not adjust the resource requests and limits of individual Pods. It would not resolve OOMKill issues caused by incorrect limits.
D: Incorrect. Node Auto Provisioning adds nodes to accommodate unschedulable Pods, but it does not fix incorrectly configured memory limits that cause OOMKills.

