Question 10

Your team maintains a monorepo containing multiple services. You want Cloud Build triggers to only run the build for the service whose code was changed, rather than rebuilding all services on every commit. What should you do?

A. Create one Cloud Build trigger per service and configure each trigger with an included files filter matching that service's directory.
B. Create a single Cloud Build trigger and use a shell script to detect changed files and conditionally run build steps.
C. Split the monorepo into separate repositories so that each Cloud Build trigger fires only for its repository.
D. Use Cloud Scheduler to poll for repository changes and trigger builds accordingly.

Correct answer
A. Create one Cloud Build trigger per service and configure each trigger with an included files filter matching that service's directory.

Feedback
A: Correct. Cloud Build triggers support included files glob patterns. When a trigger has an included files filter, it only fires if a commit changes files matching that pattern. One trigger per service directory is the recommended approach for monorepos.
B: Incorrect. A single trigger would still fire on every commit regardless of which service changed. Using shell scripts to conditionally skip steps is fragile and less maintainable.
C: Incorrect. Splitting a monorepo into multiple repos is a significant architectural change that may not be desirable and introduces other coordination challenges.
D: Incorrect. Using Cloud Scheduler to poll for changes is inefficient and introduces unnecessary latency in the build pipeline.

https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers#build_trigger_behavior

