Question 11

Your application is deployed on Cloud Run and receives intermittent spikes in traffic. The application occasionally returns HTTP 429 Too Many Requests errors during spikes. You need to reduce these errors with minimal code changes. What should you do?

A. Increase the maximum number of container instances in the Cloud Run service configuration.
B. Enable Cloud CDN in front of Cloud Run to cache responses.
C. Increase the concurrency setting of the Cloud Run service to allow each container instance to handle more simultaneous requests.
D. Configure a Cloud Tasks queue to buffer incoming requests.

Correct answer
A. Increase the maximum number of container instances in the Cloud Run service configuration.

Feedback
A: Correct. Cloud Run automatically scales the number of container instances to handle traffic. If the maximum instance limit is too low, requests are queued or rejected with 429 errors. Increasing the maximum instance limit allows Cloud Run to scale out further during spikes.
B: Incorrect. Cloud CDN caches static content but does not help with dynamic request processing capacity. It would not reduce 429 errors for non-cacheable requests.
C: Incorrect. Increasing concurrency may help if instances are underutilized, but if the service is already CPU-bound, increasing concurrency per instance will not help and could increase latency. The primary lever for capacity is the instance count.
D: Incorrect. Cloud Tasks adds complexity and latency. It is appropriate for asynchronous workloads but not for real-time user-facing traffic reduction of 429 errors.

https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/about-concurrency
https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/max-instances

