Question 13

Your team needs to safely store and rotate API keys used by a Cloud Run application. The application must be able to retrieve the latest key at runtime. You want to follow the principle of least privilege. What should you do?

A. Store the API keys in environment variables in the Cloud Run service configuration.
B. Store the API keys in a Cloud Storage bucket and grant the Cloud Run service account read access to the bucket.
C. Store the API keys in Secret Manager and grant the Cloud Run service account the Secret Manager Secret Accessor role on those specific secrets.
D. Encode the API keys in Base64 and embed them in the container image.

Correct answer
C. Store the API keys in Secret Manager and grant the Cloud Run service account the Secret Manager Secret Accessor role on those specific secrets.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. Environment variables in Cloud Run service configurations are visible to anyone with sufficient IAM access to the service and are not automatically rotated. They do not follow best practices for secret management.
B: Incorrect. Storing secrets in Cloud Storage is not a recommended practice. Cloud Storage objects are not designed for secret management and lack features like rotation notifications and automatic versioning designed for secrets.
C: Correct. Secret Manager is the Google-recommended service for storing secrets. Granting the Secret Manager Secret Accessor role only on specific secrets follows the principle of least privilege. Cloud Run can automatically inject secret values and supports rotation.
D: Incorrect. Embedding secrets in a container image exposes them to anyone with access to the image and makes rotation extremely difficult.

https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/secrets
https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview

