Question 16

You have a Cloud Run service that processes user-uploaded files. Occasionally, files that fail processing cause the container to crash. You need to ensure that failed files do not prevent other files from being processed and that failed files can be retried later. What should you do?

A. Configure Cloud Run with a higher request timeout and increase the number of container instances.
B. Use Cloud Tasks to enqueue file processing jobs, configure retry settings, and have Cloud Run pull tasks from the queue.
C. Add a try/catch block in the application code to log errors without crashing the container.
D. Configure Cloud Run to trigger from Pub/Sub push subscriptions and set an acknowledgment deadline with retry policy on the subscription.

Correct answer
D. Configure Cloud Run to trigger from Pub/Sub push subscriptions and set an acknowledgment deadline with retry policy on the subscription.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. A higher timeout and more instances do not address the core problem of failed files blocking others. They only delay the inevitable crash.
B: Correct (also valid). Cloud Tasks provides reliable task queuing with configurable retries and dead-letter queues, but is a pull-based model. D is the more idiomatic event-driven pattern.
C: Incorrect. Catching errors without crashing helps, but it does not provide an automatic retry mechanism for failed files.
D: Correct. Using Pub/Sub push subscriptions decouples file processing from the upload event. If Cloud Run fails to process a message (returns a non-2xx response), Pub/Sub automatically retries the message according to the retry policy, ensuring failed files are retried without blocking others.

https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/triggering/pubsub-push
https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/handling-failures

