Question 17

Your team has adopted Site Reliability Engineering practices. After a major incident you want to conduct a review that systematically identifies contributing factors without placing blame on individuals. What should you do?

A. Write an incident report that names the engineer who made the change that caused the outage and distribute it to management.
B. Conduct a blameless postmortem that focuses on systemic and process failures, documents action items, and shares learnings with the broader organization.
C. Institute a new policy requiring all changes to be reviewed by two additional engineers before deployment.
D. Have the on-call engineer document their personal mistakes and present them to the team.

Correct answer
B. Conduct a blameless postmortem that focuses on systemic and process failures, documents action items, and shares learnings with the broader organization.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. Attributing blame to individuals discourages transparency and does not address the systemic issues that led to the incident. It is counter to SRE practices.
B: Correct. Blameless postmortems are a core SRE practice. They focus on what systems, processes, and tooling contributed to the incident, not on who made a mistake. This encourages psychological safety and leads to lasting systemic improvements.
C: Incorrect. Adding more review steps is one possible action item but is not the same as conducting a blameless postmortem to understand the full scope of contributing factors.
D: Incorrect. Having an engineer document personal mistakes and present them publicly creates blame and discourages transparency.

