Question 99

Your GKE application experiences slow Pod startup during scale-out events because each new Pod downloads a large ML model file from Cloud Storage on startup. You want to reduce Pod startup time. What should you do?

A. Increase the size of GKE nodes so fewer Pods need to start at the same time.
B. Pre-bake the ML model file into the container image so it is included in the image layers and does not need to be downloaded at startup.
C. Use an init container to download the ML model and cache it in an emptyDir volume shared with the main container.
D. Configure a Kubernetes DaemonSet to pre-download the ML model to each node's local disk and mount it as a hostPath volume.

Correct answer
B. Pre-bake the ML model file into the container image so it is included in the image layers and does not need to be downloaded at startup.

Feedback
A: Incorrect. Larger nodes do not reduce per-Pod startup time. They allow more Pods to run on fewer nodes but do not eliminate the model download latency.
B: Correct. Including the ML model file in the container image means it is pulled once when the image is first downloaded to a node (leveraging image layer caching) and is immediately available to all Pods on that node without individual downloads, significantly reducing startup time.
C: Incorrect. An init container that downloads the model still requires each Pod to download the model individually on every startup.
D: Incorrect. A DaemonSet with hostPath volumes is operationally complex and creates tight coupling between Pods and specific nodes. It also does not work well with Cluster Autoscaler since new nodes would not have the model until the DaemonSet Pod runs.

